# OSS Verify `spirv-tools/spvtools_binary_parser_fuzzer`: its statements, on the pinned riscv64 builds

Generated by `tools/verification-projects/upstream_statements.py`; do not edit.

`projects/spirv-tools/spvtools_binary_parser_fuzzer/Statements.v` of `theorem-labs/oss-verify-proofs` @ `98e995d4`
(SHA-256 `88c5452d5708540b54921aa5fb5c93185dcf14811ee002ed5d4b9f6e2bdda5bb`), stated over
`Silmaril.Process.LPS.Shapes` on `Machines.riscvLps` (LPS's own RV64GC machine).

Each definition `S` takes the four memory images (`Upstream.Images4`): it is a family
over all images, and only its instance at the real images (`elf-snapshot`'s images of
the ELFs that `pins` names by SHA-256, from `index/binaries.tsv`) is the upstream
statement. `pins.Admits I` fixes the layout and byte counts of the images, not their
bytes, so `∃ I, pins.Admits I ∧ S I` is *not* the upstream statement. The safe forms
are a proof of `S` at the real images (the data layer's `images`) or of
`∀ I, pins.Admits I → S I`, which is stronger. The images are not in the repository
(about 290 MB of ELF for the 60 entry points): the data layer that
`upstream_statements.py gen --data-dir` writes loads them (`include_bytes`), checks
each against its pin's segment SHA-256s (in the generator) and shape (in Lean), and
instantiates these definitions at them.

Proved in the release (`proofs/spirv-tools/spvtools_binary_parser_fuzzer/*/proof.yaml`):
`asan_agrees`, `ubsan_agrees`.
The bound statements (`_disagrees_at_most`) use `Distribution.v`, which is not
translated, so they are *untranslated*: listed in `statements` as data, with no Lean
proposition (`Problem.denote?` is `none`, `<name>_untranslated`), neither provable
nor refutable. In particular they do not denote `False` (upstream's holds at
`b = 1`). Every other statement is a definition `S` with `denote? = some S`
(`<name>_eq`, by `rfl`).
-/

